machine learning is everywhere you look  and it's made of put different pieces  when building a model you may not be  thinking about the infrastructure needed  to train and serve it and that's why  coop flow exists let's talk about what  queue flow is trying to solve and how  you can use it  [Applause]  welcome to cube lo 101 today we'll be  giving an introduction to keep flow if  you want to check out the documentation  directly check out the link below to  read more about cube flow now when it  comes to maintaining machine learning  systems it can be especially challenging  to manage all the application platform  and resource considerations ml apps have  a different footprint from other web or  mobile deployments for example a  training phase is resource intensive  while an inference phase is lightweight  and speedy you'll also need the tools  and frameworks to run all of these  pieces existing solutions that are great  for building applications and other  domains don't capture the nuances needed  4 ml so key flow is all about creating a  standard for deploying enterprise ml  apps not just development or production  but the entire lifecycle cube flow is an  open source project that contains a  curated set of compatible tools and  frameworks specific for ml one of the  primary goals for cube flow is to make  it easy for everyone to develop deploy  and manage portable scaleable machine  learning it may not surprise you to hear  it's built on top of kubernetes an open  source platform for running and  orchestrating containers kubernetes is  built to run consistently across  different environments and this is key  to how key flow operates let's talk  about three principles that keep low is  built around composability Portability  and scalability composability means that  you can choose what's right for you on  your project people starting out in  machine learning tend to be focused on  the end result of the model but there's  more than just building a model the  process that goes into an ml project has  many stages and each stage is made up of  different building blocks that you  choose it shouldn't matter if you have  special requirements for your ml  workflow like if you need to use  different versions of tensorflow  for different parts of your project cube  flow extends kubernetes ability to run  independent and configurable steps with  machine learning specific frameworks and  libraries portability means you can run  all the pieces of your  project anywhere you're running cute  loom so it handles the platform  specifics and you can just focus on your  model write your code once and let Q  Blow handle the abstraction so you can  run it on your laptop and on-prem  training rig and in the cloud  scalability means your projects can  access more resources when they're  needed and release them when they're not  every environment can have different  computing resources like CPUs GPUs and  TP use but you may also need to scale  across teams and running experiments q  flow leverages the abilities built into  kubernetes to maximize available  resources and scale with as little  manual effort as possible so if you're  familiar with kubernetes you can  probably see how it enables cube flow to  do all of these but there's also a lot  of complexity in knowing how kubernetes  works which can require expertise and  many additional concepts another option  may be hosted mo offerings but that may  not work if you have on-prem hardware  requirements q flow is designed to let  kubernetes do what it's good at and  allow you to focus on defining and  running ML workflows including managing  data running notebooks training models  and serving them it's also open source  and has a growing community now that's a  pretty high-level overview of what q  flow is in the next video we'll go  through setting it up and running an  example but you can also try it out for  yourself on google cloud by checking out  the link below and visiting q org to  read mo